well let's talk now to the trade  secretary Liam Fox who joins us from  central London good morning to you mr.  Fox good morning it is the eve of the  vote tomorrow do you think the  government will win it well the numbers  at the moment don't look particularly  favorable but the Prime Minister will be  requests to make a statement in the  House of Commons this afternoon setting  out the discussions she's had in recent  days with European leaders and the  reassurances that she's been able to get  on some of the issues that MPs are  concerned about notably around the eye  the backstop which is of course the  insurance policy that we have in case  we're unable to come to an agreement  with the European Union over the period  of the implementation period in the next  two years so numbers you admit and not  looking good at the moment so as it  stands before the prime minister utters  a word today looks like you'll lose well  we have to wait and see it depends on  our our MPs interprets what the Prime  Minister is going to say and we've got a  clear instruction from the voters which  is that we have to leave the European  Union Parliament gave that decision to  the people the people took that decision  Parliament must now have the humility to  carry that decision out in fact I would  say it's a matter of duty in a matter of  honor that we do so the question is how  do we do that now in front of us we have  the deal that the Prime Minister is  negotiated with European leaders it's  not the deal that everybody would want  but then probably no negotiation is but  it's a way in which we can carry out the  wishes of the British people with  minimal disruption in terms of our  economy the two other alternatives are  to leave with no deal at all or indeed  not to leave the European Union now I  think leaving with no deal while I think  it's entirely survivable for the UK  economy would be problematic in the  short term but I think no brexit would  create such a chasm between Parliament  and the voters that its consequences  would be unknowable yea the Prime  Minister is pulling out all the stops  today at the last minute on the eve of  the vote as she was to try and persuade  MP she's got the speech up in stoke  today and she's also making that  statement in the Commons on the Irish  border but as you mentioned there  the possibility of no brexit at all has  started to be talked about and the Prime  Minister is going to reference that in  her speech in stoke-on-trent today do  you think that's the likely outcome if  MPs reject her plans tomorrow I think  it's a possible outcome it's very clear  that there are a number of MPs who think  that they can defy the will of the  people they think that their opinions  seem to be more important than the  people who voted in the referendum I  take huge issue with that I think as I  said that we have a duty to carry out  the result of the referendum but there's  in it we have a parliament for the  majority of MPs themselves voted to  remain the question is whether they will  put their own opinions ahead of the  opinions of the seventeen point four  million people who voted to leave the EU  and you've said before that something  can be 5050 if this deal doesn't go  through  no brexit what are the odds on that in  your opinion then if this deal doesn't  get through how likely is it looking  it's impossible to say because it's tied  up with a lot of parliamentary procedure  but it certainly is possible because  there are quite a number of MPs who  would like that to be the outcome now I  think that you know when we had the  general election in 2017 both major  parties said that they would respect the  result of the referendum I think of MPs  individually now turned around and said  actually we're not even going to honor  our own manifestos nevermind the result  of the referendum itself I think you  will see a huge gulf between members of  parliament and the public and it's hard  to see how you would repair that damage  in terms of Parliament's reputation a  rebel MPs plotting against you there was  all that coverage in the Sunday papers  yesterday about mp's wrestling to take  control from the government in  Parliament to have a say on brexit Boris  Johnson in his column in The Telegraph  today has said that this plot is  nonsense what what are your views on MPs  actions to try and control the outcome  of what's going on oh it's not nonsense  there is definitely a group of MPs who  would want that to happen and I think  that we need to be realistic about the  potential options and should the Prime  Minister be unable to persuade  Parliament on her deal so to simply  dismiss it as unrealistic I know from  conversations that I've had that there  are a number of MPs who don't want  bricks it to happen at all some of whom  won't even be standing at the next  election so don't really care about the  electoral consequences to dismiss it I  think is not responsible okay I just  want to put some viewers questions to  you if I may I put out a tweet last  night we got lots of questions in for  you and this has come in from Macaulay  Mosley he said can we please ask him Fox  if he honestly believes that Theresa  Mae's deal will survive the meaningful  vote or is this a ploy the government  are using to run down the clock on the  brexit because you must emit all Tereza  May's actions are coming in the 24 hours  before this meaningful vote well I don't  believe that to be true the government  has been making a lot of attempts to  persuade members of parliament we had  had a number of days of debate on all  the issues across all the governmental  departments I'm leaving for the  government today on global Britain and  trade so there's been substantial debate  on this but we do want to come to a  conclusion and I do hope that tomorrow  MPs will bring this process to an end by  voting for the Prime Minister's deal if  we don't they'll be further uncertainty  yeah nothing about this breakfast  process is as easy as it may have looked  at the outset this is coming from Terry  boy on Twitter he says does Liam Fox  plan to apologise to voters after his  assurance for getting a deal with the EU  would be one of the easiest things in  human history what I said was the trade  agreement that we come to for our future  trading relationship with the European  Union should technically be easy because  we begin with exactly the same  regulations and exactly the same  legislation which we don't in any other  trade saying that but we're not but  we're not discussing the trade agreement  at the moment were discussing with  withdrawal agreements we cannot get the  negotiations on the future trading  relationship until we have their  withdrawal agreements in place that is  the key point and this has been about  the politics of it not the future  trading relationship  and this is from Sarah McEvoy she says  the public voted to leave the EU but  Teresa Mays deal ties us to them forever  the complete opposite of what we the  people voted for surely Parliament  cannot vote for a deal that goes totally  against what the public voted for there  has been one criticism hasn't it in  terms of Teresa Mays plan tying us to  the EU more then perhaps voters would  like well I don't believe it does that  but I've read these criticisms but or on  detailed questioning and do we have an  end to free movement and control of our  own borders yes we do do we have an end  to the oversight of the European Court  in terms of the British law yes we do  do we stop paying vast amounts of money  into the European Union after the end of  our implementation period yes we do in  my view it does give us the things that  I argued for and fought for in the very  long period when I was campaigning to  leave the European Union okay well thank  you to our viewers for tweeting in their  questions and thank you to you Liam Fox  for answering them just one before we go  we've just been looking t